---
title: "Example: terrain, and more of Pernambuco"
description: >
  What an elevation raster changes in walking and cycling times: the Recife
  metropolitan region as one network, five more municipalities of
  Pernambuco, and two cities of hills, Salvador and Olinda, with the
  Copernicus elevation model checked against the ground first: at 30 m it
  reads the roofs, and a flat district shows how much that costs.
---

```{r, include = FALSE}
# This article is precomputed: its source is terrain.Rmd.orig, knitted into
# terrain.Rmd by `bash site/figures/make.sh --terrain` (the data takes
# hundreds of megabytes of downloads and osmium, too much for every site
# build). Only the chunks written out below by cat() run when pkgdown
# renders it.
knitr::opts_chunk$set(collapse = TRUE, comment = "#>")
work <- file.path(tempdir(), "terrain")
dir.create(work, showWarnings = FALSE)
if (nzchar(Sys.getenv("PEABIRU_PE_CACHE")) && !file.exists(file.path(work, "pe-data"))) {
  file.symlink(Sys.getenv("PEABIRU_PE_CACHE"), file.path(work, "pe-data"))
}
knitr::opts_knit$set(root.dir = work)
local({
  hook <- knitr::knit_hooks$get("output")
  real <- normalizePath(file.path(work, "pe-data"), mustWork = FALSE)
  knitr::knit_hooks$set(output = function(x, options) hook(gsub(real, "pe-data", x, fixed = TRUE), options))
})
live <- function(code, opts = "echo = FALSE") {
  cat("```{r, ", opts, "}\n", code, "\n```\n", sep = "")
}
```

```{r, echo = FALSE, results = "asis"}
live('source("setup.R")', "include = FALSE")
```

The [Recife and Caruaru example](recife-caruaru.html) routes on streets
without elevation. This one adds an elevation raster and asks what it
changes, on foot and by bicycle:

- in **Pernambuco**: Recife, Olinda, Jaboatão dos Guararapes, Caruaru,
  Garanhuns and Petrolina, each on its own network, and the **Região
  Metropolitana do Recife** (14 municipalities, 3.7 million people) as one
  network, where the question is also how many residents have their nearest
  school or health unit across a municipal border;
- in two **cities of hills**: Salvador (Bahia), split by an escarpment
  between its upper and lower towns, and Olinda, whose historic centre sits
  on hills beside a flat coastal strip.

The short version: slopes matter, less than a first run suggests. The open
elevation model at 30 m reads the tops of buildings, and on a flat street
between towers that alone costs as much as the hills do elsewhere. The page
measures that before anything else, and then uses the 90 m version of the
model, whose results are on the low side.

The data steps are those of the Recife and Caruaru example, kept as
functions in
[`site/figures/pernambuco-data.R`](https://github.com/StrategicProjects/peabiru-routing/blob/main/site/figures/pernambuco-data.R),
which this page sources. The output shown is what the code prints; it was
computed on 2026-11-12.

**Streets only.** No open GTFS feed was found for Recife or for Salvador:
neither Pernambuco nor Bahia has a feed in the Mobility Database catalogue
(read on 2026-11-12), and a search for "gtfs" on the open-data portal of the
city of Recife returns nothing. So there is no public transport here.

## The data

| Data | Source | Licence |
|---|---|---|
| Streets | [OpenStreetMap](https://www.openstreetmap.org/copyright), extract of the Nordeste region by [Geofabrik](https://download.geofabrik.de/south-america/brazil/nordeste.html), dated 2026-11-01 (445 MB, MD5 `93318fcddd85a53e8386eaa93588847c`) | ODbL 1.0 |
| Elevation | [Copernicus DEM](https://registry.opendata.aws/copernicus-dem/), GLO-90 (3 arc seconds, about 90 m) and, for comparison, GLO-30 (1 arc second): a surface model, 2021 release, files of 2022-05-09, read from the tiles on the AWS open data registry on 2026-11-12 | [Copernicus DEM licence](https://dataspace.copernicus.eu/explore-data/data-collections/copernicus-contributing-missions/collections-description/COP-DEM): free of charge, with the attribution at the end of this page |
| Municipal boundaries | [IBGE, malhas municipais](https://servicodados.ibge.gov.br/api/docs/malhas?versao=3) (API, version 3) | public data, IBGE |
| Population | [IBGE, Censo Demográfico 2022, census tracts with their population](https://ftp.ibge.gov.br/Censos/Censo_Demografico_2022/Agregados_por_Setores_Censitarios/malha_com_atributos/setores/gpkg/UF/), variable `v0001`, files of Pernambuco (44 MB) and Bahia (118 MB) | public data, IBGE |
| Schools | INEP, Catálogo de Escolas 2023, with the coordinates of each school, as published by Ipea's [geobr](https://ipeagit.github.io/geobr/) (`schools_2023`, 101 MB for Brazil) | public data, INEP; geobr is MIT |
| Health units | [CNES](https://cnes.datasus.gov.br/), Ministério da Saúde, through [OpenDataSUS](https://opendatasus.saude.gov.br/) (`cnes_estabelecimentos_csv.zip`, 57 MB for Brazil, updated daily; downloaded on 2026-11-12) | open data, Ministério da Saúde |

`prepare_city()` cuts the streets of a municipality (or of the 14 of the
metropolitan region) with a buffer around it, puts the census population on
a hexagonal grid (cells 500 m apart; 250 m in the small municipality of
Olinda) and lists the public schools in operation and the primary health
care units (CNES types 1 and 2) in the cut area. `prepare_dem()` crops the
elevation model over the box of those streets, on the model's own grid.
Both need [osmium](https://osmcode.org/osmium-tool/); the crop uses GDAL
through sf, and the checks below use terra.

```{r prepare, message = FALSE}
library(peabiru)
library(sf)
options(timeout = 3600)

# the data steps of the Recife and Caruaru example, as functions:
# prepare_city() and prepare_dem()
pipeline <- Sys.getenv("PEABIRU_PE_SCRIPT", paste0(
  "https://raw.githubusercontent.com/StrategicProjects/peabiru-routing/main/",
  "site/figures/pernambuco-data.R"))
Sys.setenv(PEABIRU_PE_CACHE = Sys.getenv("PEABIRU_EXAMPLE_DATA", "pe-data"))
source(pipeline)
data_dir <- pe_cache()

keys <- c("recife", "olinda", "jaboatao", "caruaru", "garanhuns", "petrolina",
          "rmr", "salvador")
summary_of <- function(k) {
  dir <- prepare_city(k)
  prepare_dem(k, arcsec = 3)       # GLO-90, into <city>/network-dem90/
  cells <- read.csv(file.path(dir, "cells.csv"))
  data.frame(key = k,
             extract_MB = round(file.size(file.path(dir, "network", paste0(k, ".osm.pbf"))) / 1e6, 1),
             dem_MB = round(file.size(file.path(dir, "network-dem90", paste0(k, "_dem90.tif"))) / 1e6, 1),
             cells = sum(cells$population > 0),
             people = round(sum(cells$population)),
             schools = nrow(read.csv(file.path(dir, "schools.csv"))),
             health_units = nrow(read.csv(file.path(dir, "health.csv"))))
}
do.call(rbind, lapply(keys, summary_of))
```

`people` is the population of the Censo 2022 in the cells; `cells`, the
cells with residents. Schools and health units include those in the buffer
around each area (10 km around Recife, 3 km around the metropolitan region,
Olinda and Salvador, 5 km around the others), so a trip can end across the
border. Petrolina faces Juazeiro, in Bahia, across the São Francisco: its
health units are read from the CNES rows of both states. In Salvador's
buffer there is mostly sea.

## The elevation model, checked against the ground

The Copernicus DEM is a *surface* model: radar measured the top of what it
saw, roofs and tree crowns included. It comes at 1 arc second (GLO-30, about
30 m) and, averaged by its publisher, at 3 (GLO-90). peabiru samples the
raster every 10 m along each street, at the nearest pixel, so a step
between two pixels becomes a slope on one 10 m stretch: a real hillside is
read as a staircase, and a roof next to a street as a wall. Three checks,
before any routing.

**Voids, the sea.** Neither raster has a void over these cities. The sea
has no tiles and is taken as 0 m, as the registry's documentation says; a
few pixels (water, mangrove) are below zero, down to -17 m.

```{r dem}
dem_of <- function(k, arcsec = 3) {
  prepare_dem(k, arcsec = arcsec)
  tag <- if (arcsec == 1) "" else "90"
  terra::rast(file.path(data_dir, k, paste0("network-dem", tag), paste0(k, "_dem", tag, ".tif")))
}
r <- dem_of("rmr")
v <- terra::values(r, mat = FALSE)
c(res_arcsec = terra::res(r)[1] * 3600, voids = sum(is.na(v) | v < -1000),
  sea_share = round(mean(v == 0), 3), below_0_share = round(mean(v < 0), 4),
  min_m = round(min(v), 1), max_m = round(max(v), 1))
```

**A flat district.** Boa Viagem, in Recife, is a coastal plain a few metres
above the sea, lined by residential towers. Its streets, sampled as peabiru
samples them:

```{r dem-flat}
# street samples every 10 m at the nearest pixel
streets_z <- function(k, arcsec = 3, bridges = FALSE) {
  lines <- suppressWarnings(st_read(file.path(data_dir, k, "network", paste0(k, ".osm.pbf")),
                                    "lines", quiet = TRUE))
  lines <- lines[!is.na(lines$highway), ]
  is_bridge <- grepl('"bridge"=>"(yes|viaduct)"', lines$other_tags)
  lines <- st_transform(lines[if (bridges) is_bridge else !is_bridge, ], 31985)
  lines <- lines[as.numeric(st_length(lines)) >= 20, ]
  pts <- st_line_sample(lines, density = 1 / 10, type = "regular")
  n <- vapply(pts, function(p) if (st_is_empty(p)) 0L else nrow(st_coordinates(p)), 1L)
  ll <- sf_project(st_crs(31985), st_crs(4326), st_coordinates(pts)[, 1:2])
  data.frame(way = rep(lines$osm_id, n), lon = ll[, 1], lat = ll[, 2],
             z = terra::extract(dem_of(k, arcsec), ll, method = "simple")[, 1])
}
in_boa_viagem <- function(lon, lat) lon > -34.925 & lon < -34.885 & lat > -8.145 & lat < -8.09

sapply(c("GLO-30" = 1, "GLO-90" = 3), function(arcsec) {
  s <- streets_z("recife", arcsec)
  bv <- s[in_boa_viagem(s$lon, s$lat), ]
  same_way <- bv$way[-1] == bv$way[-nrow(bv)]
  c(samples = nrow(bv), round(quantile(bv$z, c(0.1, 0.5, 0.9, 0.99)), 1),
    steps_over_2m = round(mean(abs(diff(bv$z))[same_way] > 2), 3))
})
```

Half of the samples read 4.5 m or more and one in ten more than 9 m, on a
plain by the sea: the higher readings are roofs and trees. On GLO-30, 7.5% of the 10 m
steps along a street change by more than 2 m, a slope of 20% that is not
there; on GLO-90, 2.5%. What that does to travel times is measured
[below](#which-raster-a-flat-district-as-control).

**Bridges.** A surface model should see the deck of a bridge; a 90 m pixel
mostly sees what is under it. For each bridge or viaduct of the
metropolitan region, how far the lowest sample falls below the average of
its two ends:

```{r dem-bridges}
b <- streets_z("rmr", bridges = TRUE)
sag <- sapply(split(b$z, b$way), function(z) {
  if (length(z) < 3) NA else mean(z[c(1, length(z))]) - min(z[-c(1, length(z))])
})
sag <- sag[!is.na(sag)]
c(bridges = length(sag), dip_over_2m = round(mean(sag > 2), 3),
  dip_over_5m = round(mean(sag > 5), 3), worst_m = round(max(sag), 1))
```

One bridge in five dips by more than 2 m in the raster, and a few by more
than 5 m (the viaducts of Avenida Caxangá, in Recife, by almost 9 m): those
cross a valley that the deck does not go down into, and walking or cycling
over them is costed as if it did. They are few among 369 thousand street
edges, but an analysis of one corridor should look at its bridges.

## Networks with and without elevation

The same extract is read from `network/`, with no raster, and from
`network-dem90/`, where the raster turns slope costs on (`"TOBLER"`, the
default, or `"MINETTI"`); `"TOBLER30"` below is Tobler on GLO-30, from
`network-dem/`. Car times do not change with elevation.

```{r networks}
networks <- list()
network <- function(k, elevation = "NONE") {
  id <- paste(k, elevation)
  if (is.null(networks[[id]])) {
    if (elevation == "TOBLER30") prepare_dem(k)        # GLO-30, into network-dem/
    dir <- file.path(data_dir, k, switch(elevation, NONE = "network", TOBLER30 = "network-dem",
                                         "network-dem90"))
    networks[[id]] <<- build_network(dir, elevation = sub("30$", "", elevation))
  }
  networks[[id]]
}
system.time(network("rmr"))[["elapsed"]]
system.time(network("rmr", "TOBLER"))[["elapsed"]]
network("rmr", "TOBLER")
```

The metropolitan region is one extract of 21 MB and builds in about a
second, with or without the raster (the times above are of a busy laptop,
with the default number of threads).

**For the browser demo, it is too large.** The demo of the documentation
site takes extracts under 3 MB (Recife's municipality is 2.8 MB). Stripped
the same way (no buildings, no object metadata), the 14 municipalities are
8.9 MB; with nothing but the streets and the turn restrictions, 5.3 MB. So
the region is not in the demo; a dashboard would load it on a server, or by
municipality.

## Which raster: a flat district as control

The time from every cell to the nearest school and health unit is the
measure of the Recife and Caruaru example. In Boa Viagem slopes should cost
nothing, so whatever a raster adds there is the raster's own doing:

```{r nearest}
grid_of <- function(k) {
  g <- read.csv(file.path(data_dir, k, "cells.csv"), colClasses = c(id = "character"))
  g[g$population > 0, ]
}
units_of <- function(k, what) {
  read.csv(file.path(data_dir, k, paste0(what, ".csv")), colClasses = c(id = "character"))
}
# the time from each cell to the nearest school and health unit, and which one
nearest <- function(k, elevation, mode, max_lts = 2) {
  out <- grid_of(k)
  for (what in c("schools", "health")) {
    m <- travel_time_matrix(network(k, elevation), out, units_of(k, what), mode = mode,
                            max_lts = max_lts, max_trip_duration = 60)
    m <- m[order(m$from_id, m$travel_time_p50, m$to_id), ]
    m <- m[!duplicated(m$from_id), ]
    i <- match(out$id, m$from_id)
    out[[what]] <- m$travel_time_p50[i]        # NA: none within the hour
    out[[paste0(what, "_id")]] <- m$to_id[i]
  }
  out
}
share <- function(t, what, minutes = 15) {
  round(100 * sum(t$population[!is.na(t[[what]]) & t[[what]] <= minutes]) / sum(t$population), 1)
}

# what a raster adds to the walk to the nearest health unit, in minutes
# averaged over the residents, and the share of them who lose 2 or more
added <- function(k, elevation, inside = function(lon, lat) TRUE) {
  flat <- nearest(k, "NONE", "WALK")
  hill <- nearest(k, elevation, "WALK")
  d <- hill$health - flat$health
  ok <- !is.na(d) & inside(flat$lon, flat$lat)
  c(residents = round(sum(flat$population[inside(flat$lon, flat$lat)])),
    minutes_added = round(weighted.mean(d[ok], flat$population[ok]), 2),
    lose_2_or_more = round(100 * sum(flat$population[ok & d >= 1.5]) / sum(flat$population[ok]), 1))
}
rbind("Boa Viagem, GLO-30" = added("recife", "TOBLER30", in_boa_viagem),
      "Boa Viagem, GLO-90" = added("recife", "TOBLER", in_boa_viagem),
      "Recife, GLO-30" = added("recife", "TOBLER30"),
      "Recife, GLO-90" = added("recife", "TOBLER"),
      "Salvador, GLO-30" = added("salvador", "TOBLER30"),
      "Salvador, GLO-90" = added("salvador", "TOBLER"),
      "Petrolina, GLO-30" = added("petrolina", "TOBLER30"),
      "Petrolina, GLO-90" = added("petrolina", "TOBLER"))
```

On GLO-30 the flat district loses as much as the whole of Recife, hills
included: a minute on average, and a quarter of its 206 thousand residents
lose two minutes or more. That is the roofs, not the ground. On GLO-90 the
same district loses 0.15 of a minute and 1% of its residents lose two
minutes: the control is close to zero, and what remains elsewhere is mostly
terrain. Petrolina, flat and low-built, hardly changes on either.

So **everything below uses GLO-90**. It errs the other way: a pixel of 90 m
averages short, steep streets (a 30 m pixel already does), so the slope
costs here are on the low side, and the 30 m figures are a ceiling that
includes the roofs: in Salvador, 1.3 and 2.3 minutes. A bare-earth terrain
model at a finer resolution would settle it. Pernambuco has a state-wide
LiDAR survey (Pernambuco Tridimensional, PE3D); its terms of use were not
checked for this page, and it is not used here.

## Six municipalities, flat and with slopes

```{r shares}
pe <- c("recife", "olinda", "jaboatao", "caruaru", "garanhuns", "petrolina")
shares <- do.call(rbind, lapply(pe, function(k) {
  do.call(rbind, lapply(c("WALK", "BICYCLE"), function(mode) {
    flat <- nearest(k, "NONE", mode)
    hill <- nearest(k, "TOBLER", mode)
    data.frame(city = k, mode = mode,
               school_flat = share(flat, "schools"), school_slopes = share(hill, "schools"),
               health_flat = share(flat, "health"), health_slopes = share(hill, "health"))
  }))
}))
shares
```

The share of the residents (in %) within 15 minutes of the nearest public
school and primary health care unit, on flat ground and with Tobler's slope
costs. On foot every municipality loses something, up to 4 points: Jaboatão
dos Guararapes the most to a health unit (81.1% to 77.8%) and Garanhuns,
on the Borborema plateau, the most to a school (78.7% to 74.9%); Petrolina,
on the plain of the São Francisco, less than one. By bicycle the largest
change is 1.3 points: almost everyone who is within 15 minutes on flat
ground stays within it.

```{r, echo = FALSE, results = "asis"}
live('fig("terrain-pe-shares.svg")', 'echo = FALSE, fig.alt = "Dot plots of the share of residents within 15 minutes of the nearest public school and primary health care unit, walking and cycling, on flat ground and with slopes, for six municipalities of Pernambuco: on foot each loses up to 4 percentage points, by bicycle about one at most."')
```

### Recife and Caruaru, as published

The [Recife and Caruaru example](recife-caruaru.html) is computed without
elevation, and stays that way (its figures and its Python page are the same
analysis). Here are its headline numbers, the share of the population
within 15 and 30 minutes, recomputed with the slopes:

```{r recife-caruaru}
do.call(rbind, lapply(c("recife", "caruaru"), function(k) {
  do.call(rbind, lapply(c("WALK", "BICYCLE"), function(mode) {
    flat <- nearest(k, "NONE", mode)
    hill <- nearest(k, "TOBLER", mode)
    data.frame(city = k, mode = mode,
               school_15 = share(hill, "schools"), health_15 = share(hill, "health"),
               school_30 = share(hill, "schools", 30), health_30 = share(hill, "health", 30),
               flat_school_30 = share(flat, "schools", 30), flat_health_30 = share(flat, "health", 30))
  }))
}))
```

With the slopes, on foot, Recife goes from 95.6% to 95.0% of its residents
within 15 minutes of a school and from 75.8% to 73.7% of a health unit;
Caruaru from 72.5% to 70.6% and from 68.0% to 65.8%. Within 30 minutes
almost nothing changes (Recife's health units: 97.5% to 97.3%; Caruaru's:
85.7% to 84.6%), and by bicycle the largest change is 1.3 points (Caruaru's
schools within 15 minutes). The published page said that Recife is flat and
Caruaru hilly, so that Caruaru's times were the optimistic ones. Measured,
the two lose about the same: Recife's plain is flat, but the hills of the
north and the west of the city are not, and people live on them, as the map
of the next section shows. The Recife and Caruaru pages now say so.

## The metropolitan region as one network

The 14 municipalities of the Região Metropolitana do Recife as a single
network: Recife, Olinda, Jaboatão dos Guararapes, Paulista, Camaragibe, São
Lourenço da Mata, Cabo de Santo Agostinho, Ipojuca, Moreno, Igarassu, Abreu
e Lima, Itapissuma, Ilha de Itamaracá and Araçoiaba, with 3 km around them.

**The municipal border.** A municipal network stops at the border, or
needs a buffer chosen by hand; one network of the region does not. How many
residents have a school or a health unit across the border nearer than any
in their own municipality? Each cell's residents are split by the
municipality of their census tract, each unit belongs to the municipality
it stands in, and a resident counts when a unit elsewhere is strictly
nearer on foot (times are whole minutes; ties do not count) or when none in
their own municipality is within the hour:

```{r border}
munis <- st_read(file.path(data_dir, "rmr", "boundary.geojson"), quiet = TRUE)
unit_muni <- function(units) {
  p <- st_as_sf(units, coords = c("lon", "lat"), crs = 4674)
  hit <- st_intersects(st_transform(p, st_crs(munis)), munis)
  setNames(vapply(hit, function(i) if (length(i)) munis$code[i[1]] else "outside", ""), units$id)
}
# the residents of each cell, by the municipality of their census tract
by_muni <- read.csv(file.path(data_dir, "rmr", "cells_muni.csv"),
                    colClasses = c(id = "character", code = "character"))
by_muni <- by_muni[by_muni$id %in% grid_of("rmr")$id, ]
across <- function(what) {
  units <- units_of("rmr", what)
  m <- travel_time_matrix(network("rmr", "TOBLER"), grid_of("rmr"), units, mode = "WALK",
                          max_trip_duration = 60)
  # the time from each cell to the nearest unit of each municipality
  best <- tapply(m$travel_time_p50, list(m$from_id, unit_muni(units)[m$to_id]), min)
  rows <- match(by_muni$id, rownames(best))
  own <- best[cbind(rows, match(by_muni$code, colnames(best)))]
  other <- vapply(seq_along(rows), function(i) {
    if (is.na(rows[i])) return(NA_real_)
    v <- best[rows[i], colnames(best) != by_muni$code[i]]
    if (all(is.na(v))) NA_real_ else min(v, na.rm = TRUE)
  }, 0)
  nearer <- !is.na(other) & (is.na(own) | other < own)
  people <- tapply(by_muni$population * nearer, by_muni$code, sum)
  total <- tapply(by_muni$population, by_muni$code, sum)
  list(region = c(people = round(sum(people)), share = round(100 * sum(people) / sum(total), 1),
                  tied = round(sum(by_muni$population[!is.na(other) & !is.na(own) & other == own]))),
       top = round(100 * sort(people / total, decreasing = TRUE)[1:4], 1))
}
codes <- c("2610707" = "Paulista", "2609600" = "Olinda", "2603454" = "Camaragibe",
           "2611606" = "Recife", "2607752" = "Itapissuma", "2606804" = "Igarassu",
           "2607901" = "Jaboatão dos Guararapes", "2613701" = "São Lourenço da Mata")
for (what in c("schools", "health")) {
  x <- across(what)
  names(x$top) <- codes[names(x$top)]
  print(x)
}
```

For 148 thousand residents of the region (4.0%) the nearest primary health
care unit on foot is in another municipality, and for 109 thousand (2.9%)
the nearest public school; another 15 and 13 thousand have one just as
near on each side. It is a matter of a few borders: Paulista (12% of its
residents for schools, 9% for health units) and Olinda (9% for health
units), which run into each other and into Recife without a gap, then
Camaragibe. In absolute numbers Recife leads for health units (55 thousand
residents, 3.7%). An analysis by municipality without a buffer would send
these residents to a farther unit; the units outside the region, within
3 km of it, count as "another municipality" too. The registers say where a
unit is, not whom it serves: health units have catchment areas, and
municipal schools enrol their own residents first.

```{r, echo = FALSE, results = "asis"}
live('fig("terrain-rmr-border.svg")', 'echo = FALSE, fig.alt = "Bars, one per municipality of the Recife metropolitan region, of the share of its residents whose nearest public school and nearest primary health care unit on foot is in another municipality: Paulista 12% and 9%, Olinda 3% and 9%, Recife 2% and 4%, and close to zero in the outer municipalities."')
```

**Where the slopes cost.** The minutes that the slopes add to the walk to
the nearest health unit, cell by cell, in the core of the region:

```{r rmr-penalty}
added("rmr", "TOBLER")
```

```{r, echo = FALSE, results = "asis"}
live('fig("terrain-rmr-penalty.svg")', 'echo = FALSE, fig.alt = "A map of Recife, Olinda, Camaragibe and the north of Jaboatão covered by hexagons coloured by the minutes that slopes add to the walk to the nearest health unit: nothing on the coastal plain, 2 to 6 minutes on the hills of the north and the west."')
```

Across the region the slopes add half a minute on average, and 8% of the
residents lose two minutes or more. They are not spread evenly: the plain
along the coast and the rivers does not change, and the hills north and
west of Recife's centre, Camaragibe and the west of Jaboatão lose 2 to 6
minutes.

## Cities of hills: Salvador and Olinda

Two centres on high ground: the Praça Municipal of Salvador, at the top of
the escarpment (the Lacerda lift goes down to the lower town from it; a
lift is not a street, and is not in the network), and the Alto da Sé of
Olinda. The areas reached from each in 10, 20 and 30 minutes, on flat
ground, with Tobler's hiking function and with Minetti's energy cost:

```{r isochrones}
centres <- list(
  salvador = data.frame(id = "Praça Municipal", lon = -38.5128, lat = -12.9740),
  olinda = data.frame(id = "Alto da Sé", lon = -34.8408, lat = -8.0136)
)
utm_of <- c(salvador = 31984, olinda = 31985)
iso <- do.call(rbind, lapply(names(centres), function(k) {
  do.call(rbind, lapply(c("WALK", "BICYCLE"), function(mode) {
    do.call(rbind, lapply(c("NONE", "TOBLER", "MINETTI"), function(e) {
      i <- isochrone(network(k, e), centres[[k]], mode = mode, cutoffs = c(10, 20, 30), zoom = 11)
      data.frame(city = k, mode = mode, elevation = e, minutes = i$isochrone,
                 km2 = round(as.numeric(st_area(st_transform(i, utm_of[[k]]))) / 1e6, 2))
    }))
  }))
}))
xtabs(km2 ~ elevation + mode + city, data = iso[iso$minutes == 30, ])
```

With Tobler's costs, the area reached in 30 minutes from the Praça Municipal
shrinks by 16% on foot (4.4 to 3.7 km²) and by 19% by bicycle (15.1 to
12.3 km²); from the Alto da Sé, by 9% and 7%. Minetti's cost takes more:
34% and 44% in Salvador. On GLO-30 the losses with Tobler would be 35% in
Salvador and 14% in Olinda, on foot: half or more of that is roofs, by the
control above.

```{r, echo = FALSE, results = "asis"}
live('fig("terrain-iso-salvador.svg")', 'echo = FALSE, fig.alt = "Four maps of central Salvador with the areas reached from the Praça Municipal within 10, 20 and 30 minutes: walking and cycling, on flat ground on the left and with slopes on the right, where the 30-minute area is 16% smaller on foot and 19% by bicycle."')
live('fig("terrain-iso-olinda.svg")', 'echo = FALSE, fig.alt = "Four maps of Olinda and the north of Recife with the areas reached from the Alto da Sé within 10, 20 and 30 minutes: walking and cycling, on flat ground on the left and with slopes on the right, where the 30-minute area is 9% smaller on foot and 7% by bicycle."')
```

### Minutes the slopes add, by cell

```{r penalty}
sapply(c(salvador = "salvador", olinda = "olinda", recife = "recife"), added, elevation = "TOBLER")
```

The walk to the nearest health unit is 1.3 minutes longer on average in
Salvador, where a third of the residents lose two minutes or more, and half
a minute longer in Olinda and in Recife (7% and 6% lose two or more). In
Salvador the cost is spread over most of the city; in Olinda it is the
hills of the west and of the historic centre, and the coastal strip does
not change.

```{r, echo = FALSE, results = "asis"}
live('fig("terrain-penalty-salvador.svg")', 'echo = FALSE, fig.alt = "Two maps of Salvador covered by hexagons coloured by the minutes that slopes add to the walk to the nearest public school and primary health care unit: one to three minutes over most of the city, more in the valleys."')
live('fig("terrain-penalty-olinda.svg")', 'echo = FALSE, fig.alt = "Two maps of Olinda covered by hexagons coloured by the minutes that slopes add to the walk to the nearest public school and primary health care unit: nothing on the coastal strip, up to six minutes on the hills of the west."')
```

### Tobler against Minetti

`elevation = "MINETTI"` replaces Tobler's hiking function by Minetti's
energy cost of walking and cycling on a gradient. The two differ visibly:

```{r models}
models <- do.call(rbind, lapply(c("salvador", "olinda", "garanhuns", "caruaru"), function(k) {
  do.call(rbind, lapply(c("WALK", "BICYCLE"), function(mode) {
    do.call(rbind, lapply(c("NONE", "TOBLER", "MINETTI"), function(e) {
      t <- nearest(k, e, mode)
      data.frame(city = k, mode = mode, elevation = e, health_15 = share(t, "health"),
                 mean_minutes = round(weighted.mean(t$health, t$population, na.rm = TRUE), 2))
    }))
  }))
}))
reshape(models, direction = "wide", idvar = c("city", "mode"), timevar = "elevation")
```

Minetti's cost is the larger in every city, on foot and by bicycle, by a
factor of two to three: in Salvador the average walk to a health unit goes
from 15.1 minutes on flat ground to 16.2 with Tobler and 17.8 with Minetti,
and the residents within 15 minutes from 60.7% to 56.4% and 49.2%. Each
average is over the residents who reach a unit within the hour with that
model, so the three are not over exactly the same cells: cell by cell, over
those that reach one both ways, Tobler adds 1.26 minutes in Salvador (the
table of the previous section), not the 1.09 that separates the two
averages. The
choice of function matters as much as the choice of raster: a result
should state both. `"TOBLER"` is the
default, as in r5r.

```{r, echo = FALSE, results = "asis"}
live('fig("terrain-models.svg")', 'echo = FALSE, fig.alt = "Dot plots, one row per city, of the average time to the nearest health unit, walking and cycling, on flat ground, with Tobler and with Minetti, for Salvador, Olinda, Garanhuns and Caruaru: Minetti is the slowest everywhere, Tobler between it and flat ground."')
```

### Traffic stress and slope

`max_lts` sets the highest level of traffic stress a cyclist rides (1,
calm streets only, to 4, any street; 2 is the default); on a street above
it the bicycle is walked. Slopes and stress add up: the share of the
residents (in %) within 15 minutes of a health unit by bicycle, and the
average time to it:

```{r lts}
lts <- do.call(rbind, lapply(c("salvador", "olinda", "recife"), function(k) {
  do.call(rbind, lapply(1:4, function(l) {
    flat <- nearest(k, "NONE", "BICYCLE", max_lts = l)
    hill <- nearest(k, "TOBLER", "BICYCLE", max_lts = l)
    minutes <- function(t) round(weighted.mean(t$health, t$population, na.rm = TRUE), 2)
    data.frame(city = k, max_lts = l, flat_15 = share(flat, "health"),
               slopes_15 = share(hill, "health"), flat_minutes = minutes(flat),
               slopes_minutes = minutes(hill))
  }))
}))
lts
```

Stress costs more than slope. In Salvador a cyclist who rides only calm
streets takes 9.5 minutes on average to the nearest health unit, and one who
rides any street 5.8; the slopes add 0.5 to 0.8 of a minute at every level.
In Recife and Olinda the slopes add 0.1 to 0.25 of a minute. Levels 2 and 3
give almost the same times in the three cities.

```{r, echo = FALSE, results = "asis"}
live('fig("terrain-lts.svg")', 'echo = FALSE, fig.alt = "Line charts, one per city, of the average cycling time to the nearest health unit as the highest level of traffic stress ridden goes from 1 to 4, on flat ground and with slopes: the time falls by a third or more from level 1 to level 4, and the slopes add under a minute in Salvador and a quarter of a minute or less in Olinda and Recife."')
```

## What is not measured

- **The elevation model** is a surface model at about 90 m. It has no voids
  here, but it smooths short steep streets and stairs, reads some roofs and
  trees still, and dips under one bridge in five; the 30 m version reads
  roofs enough to cost a flat district a minute. The slope costs on this
  page are a low estimate, not a measurement of the ground.
- **Slope costs** apply to walking and cycling times on streets; steps,
  lifts and funiculars (Salvador's Elevador Lacerda and its inclined planes)
  are not modelled, and they are how people cross the escarpment.
- **Cycling** assumes a rider on every street up to `max_lts`, with stress
  levels derived from OpenStreetMap tags; no surface, no lighting, no
  safety.
- **Nearest** is by travel time on the street network, from the centre of
  each cell (500 m apart; 250 m in Olinda), in whole minutes. It says where
  a unit is, not whether it has a place or serves that address.
- **The registers**: schools without coordinates in INEP's catalogue are
  left out, CNES coordinates are those the establishments declared, and
  CNES changes daily (this is the file of 2026-11-12). The Censo is of 2022,
  the schools of 2023, the streets of 2026-11-01.
- **No public transport and no congestion**: no open GTFS feed for Recife
  or Salvador was found, and nothing here drives.

